INSERT INTO public.qb_questions (subject_id, difficulty, question_type, question_text, options, correct_answer, explanation, points, tags) VALUES
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A quality auditor calculates process capability (Cpk) for a fabric GSM specification (target 180 GSM, tolerance ±10 GSM, i.e., spec limits 170-190) using: Cpk = min[(USL−mean)/(3σ), (mean−LSL)/(3σ)]. If the process mean is 184 GSM with standard deviation σ=2.5, what is the Cpk, and is the process considered capable (typically Cpk≥1.33 is the target for a well-controlled process)?', '["Cpk = min[(190−184)/(3×2.5), (184−170)/(3×2.5)] = min[6/7.5, 14/7.5] = min[0.8, 1.867] = 0.8 — this is BELOW the 1.33 capability target, indicating the process is NOT adequately capable despite the mean being reasonably centered","Cpk = (190−170)/(6×2.5) = 20/15 = 1.33, incorrectly using the full tolerance RANGE instead of correctly checking distance to each individual spec limit from the ACTUAL (off-center) process mean","Cpk is always exactly 1.0 whenever the process mean falls within the specification limits, regardless of the actual standard deviation","Cpk cannot be calculated without knowing the exact sample size used to estimate the standard deviation, making this calculation technically impossible from the given data"]'::jsonb, 'Cpk = min[(190−184)/(3×2.5), (184−170)/(3×2.5)] = min[6/7.5, 14/7.5] = min[0.8, 1.867] = 0.8 — this is BELOW the 1.33 capability target, indicating the process is NOT adequately capable despite the mean being reasonably centered', 'Cpk correctly takes the MINIMUM of the two one-sided capability calculations (distance to upper limit and distance to lower limit, each divided by 3σ), NOT the full-range formula (which would be Cp, not Cpk, and ignores process centering) — here, since the mean (184) is off-center, closer to the UPPER limit (190) than the lower limit (170), the UPPER-side calculation (0.8) is the binding, lower value, correctly identifying that the process is NOT capable (Cpk=0.8 < 1.33 target) even though a naive Cp-style full-range calculation would misleadingly suggest 1.33 — this distinction between Cp (ignores centering) and Cpk (accounts for centering) is a critical, commonly-confused SPC concept.', 3, '{"Cpk-calculation"}'::text[]),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A quality manager reviewing two suppliers'' AQL inspection HISTORY over the past year notices Supplier A has a 96% first-pass rate (shipments passing AQL inspection on the FIRST attempt) while Supplier B has an 89% first-pass rate — but Supplier B''s AVERAGE DEFECT COUNT per failed inspection is much lower (just 1-2 defects over the accept number) compared to Supplier A''s failed inspections (which typically show 8-10 defects over the accept number, when they do fail). What does this pattern suggest about comparing the two suppliers using first-pass rate ALONE?', '["First-pass rate alone is always a complete, fully sufficient metric for supplier comparison, making this additional defect-severity data irrelevant to any real evaluation","First-pass rate alone can be misleading — Supplier B''s LOWER first-pass rate is driven by many narrowly-marginal failures (just barely over the threshold, suggesting a process operating close to but usually within control), while Supplier A''s HIGHER first-pass rate combined with occasional severe failures (far over the threshold) could indicate a less consistent underlying process that happens to pass MOST of the time but produces genuinely bad batches when it does fail — the defect-severity pattern reveals underlying process consistency information that pass-rate alone doesn''t capture","A supplier with ANY failed inspections should always be considered strictly worse than a supplier with a higher first-pass rate, regardless of the severity or margin of those failures","Defect count over the accept number has no diagnostic value beyond simply confirming whether a shipment passed or failed, making this severity-margin information analytically useless"]'::jsonb, 'First-pass rate alone can be misleading — Supplier B''s LOWER first-pass rate is driven by many narrowly-marginal failures (just barely over the threshold, suggesting a process operating close to but usually within control), while Supplier A''s HIGHER first-pass rate combined with occasional severe failures (far over the threshold) could indicate a less consistent underlying process that happens to pass MOST of the time but produces genuinely bad batches when it does fail — the defect-severity pattern reveals underlying process consistency information that pass-rate alone doesn''t capture', 'This is an advanced statistical-quality-reasoning insight connecting to the earlier AQL-marginal-pass discussion — a simple pass/fail RATE metric discards information about HOW CLOSE each result was to the threshold; Supplier B''s pattern (frequent but narrowly-marginal failures) suggests a process operating consistently NEAR the boundary (a more predictable, controllable situation), while Supplier A''s pattern (rare but severe failures when they occur) suggests a less consistent process that''s usually fine but occasionally produces genuinely bad batches — this distinction matters practically because Supplier A''s failure pattern is arguably MORE concerning from a risk-management standpoint (occasional severe quality escapes) despite its superior headline pass-rate number, illustrating why sophisticated supplier evaluation looks beyond simple pass-rate percentages.', 3, '{"defect-severity-pattern-analysis"}'::text[]),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A textile testing lab''s Gauge R&R study on a fabric strength tester reports: Repeatability (equipment) variation = 68% of total measurement variation, Reproducibility (between-operator) variation = 22%, and true Part-to-Part (genuine product) variation = only 10%. What does this result reveal about whether this measurement SYSTEM is currently fit for making product accept/reject decisions?', '["This result confirms the measurement system is excellent and fully fit for purpose, since Gauge R&R studies always validate whatever system is being tested regardless of the actual percentage breakdown","This is a SERIOUS problem — with only 10% of total observed variation actually representing genuine PRODUCT differences, and 90% combined coming from the MEASUREMENT SYSTEM itself (equipment + operator variation), most of what appears in the test data as ''variation between samples'' is actually MEASUREMENT NOISE, not real product differences — accept/reject decisions based on this system risk being essentially arbitrary/unreliable rather than reflecting true product quality differences","A high repeatability percentage (68%) is always a GOOD sign in Gauge R&R analysis, since it means the equipment consistently repeats — this interpretation is correct and no further concern is warranted","Part-to-part variation should always be the SMALLEST component in a well-functioning Gauge R&R study, making this specific result pattern (10% part-to-part) the expected, desirable outcome"]'::jsonb, 'This is a SERIOUS problem — with only 10% of total observed variation actually representing genuine PRODUCT differences, and 90% combined coming from the MEASUREMENT SYSTEM itself (equipment + operator variation), most of what appears in the test data as ''variation between samples'' is actually MEASUREMENT NOISE, not real product differences — accept/reject decisions based on this system risk being essentially arbitrary/unreliable rather than reflecting true product quality differences', 'This directly extends the earlier Gauge R&R measurement-system-reliability discussion with a critical interpretation detail — a Gauge R&R study specifically aims to determine what PORTION of observed measurement variation is genuine PART-TO-PART (real product) variation versus MEASUREMENT SYSTEM noise (repeatability + reproducibility); when measurement system noise (68%+22%=90%) massively DOMINATES genuine product variation (only 10%), this means the test data is mostly just reflecting how INCONSISTENT the measurement process itself is, NOT real quality differences between actual products — using such an unreliable measurement system to make accept/reject decisions is genuinely dangerous, since it essentially becomes closer to a random/arbitrary decision rather than a meaningful quality judgment, directly contradicting the common misconception that ANY completed Gauge R&R study automatically validates a measurement system regardless of its actual result breakdown.', 3, '{"gauge-rr-interpretation"}'::text[]),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A quality department implementing a Pareto analysis on defect data discovers that after addressing the TOP 3 defect types (which together accounted for 78% of total defects last quarter), the NEW top 3 defect types this quarter (now that the original top 3 are fixed) only account for 45% of remaining defects, even though total defect COUNT has dropped significantly overall. What does this pattern suggest about how the quality team should approach CONTINUING improvement efforts?', '["Since the original 80/20-style concentration has weakened (from 78% down to 45% for the new top-3), the REMAINING defect population is more evenly spread across many different types rather than concentrated in a few dominant ones — this means further improvement efforts face DIMINISHING returns from the same ''attack the top few types'' strategy, and the team should recognize this shift requires potentially different tactics (e.g., broader systemic process improvements rather than continuing to chase a shrinking pool of dominant individual defect types)","The team should ALWAYS continue applying the exact same top-3-defect-type strategy indefinitely, regardless of how the underlying defect distribution shape changes over time","A weakening Pareto concentration (78%→45%) indicates the quality improvement effort has been a complete failure with zero genuine progress made, contradicting the stated significant overall defect count reduction","Pareto analysis should only ever be performed ONCE per quality improvement program, making this quarter''s re-analysis an unnecessary, wasted analytical exercise"]'::jsonb, 'Since the original 80/20-style concentration has weakened (from 78% down to 45% for the new top-3), the REMAINING defect population is more evenly spread across many different types rather than concentrated in a few dominant ones — this means further improvement efforts face DIMINISHING returns from the same ''attack the top few types'' strategy, and the team should recognize this shift requires potentially different tactics (e.g., broader systemic process improvements rather than continuing to chase a shrinking pool of dominant individual defect types)', 'This is an advanced, genuinely important insight about how Pareto analysis''s usefulness EVOLVES over successive improvement cycles — the 80/20-style concentration that makes Pareto analysis so powerful INITIALLY (a few dominant types driving most defects) naturally weakens as those dominant types get successfully addressed, leaving a more evenly-distributed ''long tail'' of remaining defect types — recognizing this SHIFT is itself valuable diagnostic information: continuing to apply the same ''identify and fix the top 3'' strategy yields diminishing returns against an increasingly even distribution, signaling that the improvement approach itself may need to evolve (e.g., toward broader systemic/preventive process changes rather than continuing to chase individual defect types) — a sophisticated understanding beyond simply re-running the same analysis mechanically every period.', 3, '{"pareto-analysis-evolution"}'::text[]),
('969f50f3-57d6-4bcd-8791-00db118d9ba0', 'advanced', 'multiple_choice', 'A quality lab''s calibration records show a tensile testing machine''s load cell calibration certificate lists an uncertainty of ±0.5% at the calibration verification points. If this machine reports a fabric''s breaking strength as exactly 500 N, and the buyer''s minimum requirement is 498 N, should the quality manager treat this as a comfortable pass?', '["Yes, absolutely — 500N exceeds 498N, and calibration uncertainty is a purely theoretical concept with zero practical relevance to any individual test result''s pass/fail interpretation","This should be treated cautiously, NOT as a comfortable pass — given the ±0.5% calibration uncertainty (±2.5N at a 500N reading), the TRUE value could genuinely be anywhere from approximately 497.5N to 502.5N, meaning the true result could actually be BELOW the 498N minimum despite the machine''s displayed reading of 500N — this measurement uncertainty must be considered specifically when a result sits close to the pass/fail threshold, not just when the raw displayed number happens to be numerically above the limit","Calibration uncertainty only applies to results that FAIL a specification, never to results that appear to pass, making this scenario''s concern about a ''passing'' result technically irrelevant","A ±0.5% uncertainty is always negligible regardless of how close the measured result sits to the specification limit, making any further consideration of this uncertainty unnecessary in any scenario"]'::jsonb, 'This should be treated cautiously, NOT as a comfortable pass — given the ±0.5% calibration uncertainty (±2.5N at a 500N reading), the TRUE value could genuinely be anywhere from approximately 497.5N to 502.5N, meaning the true result could actually be BELOW the 498N minimum despite the machine''s displayed reading of 500N — this measurement uncertainty must be considered specifically when a result sits close to the pass/fail threshold, not just when the raw displayed number happens to be numerically above the limit', 'This directly extends the earlier calibration-and-audit-findings discussion with a precise, practical calculation — a ±0.5% uncertainty at a 500N reading translates to ±2.5N (0.005×500), meaning the machine''s true output could genuinely range from 497.5N to 502.5N — since this range STRADDLES the 498N minimum requirement, a reported 500N result is actually a genuinely MARGINAL, uncertain result when measurement uncertainty is properly considered, not the ''comfortable pass'' a naive reading of ''500 > 498'' might suggest — sophisticated quality management specifically flags results falling within the measurement uncertainty band around a specification limit for additional scrutiny (retesting, tighter-uncertainty verification) rather than treating any raw passing number as automatically safe.', 3, '{"measurement-uncertainty-pass-fail"}'::text[]);
